"Faux_Pseudo" <Faux_Pseudo@yahoo.com> wrote:

> Why there are 4 different errno.h files I have no
> idea.

Usually that's done to put stuff less universally
needful at the head of the chain of #include
entries, so that the unneeded stuff can be omitted
by starting the #include chain further down the
list.

The absolutely essential stuff would thus be in the
_last_ file included, but by the way #include
entries are usually done, with the #include entries
at the top of the source code file, that turns out
to be the _first_ code of the code in the four
errno.h files that appears in the fully resolved
(all #includes accomplished) source file fed from
the C preprocessor to the C compiler proper.

The intent is to avoid name collisions by the
earlier declarations, where possible, with names the
programmer invents for himself/herself, by putting
as little "include-ed code as possible into the C
preprocessor's output source code. There is also a
possiblity to make the #include linear chain a tree,
instead, so that alternative errno.h source files
could be #include-ed at the early end of the chain,
but that doesn't seem to be the case, from your
description. Another motivation is code modularity,
keeping the declarations that support a certain
layer of the code in a directory together, but not
including code there which not a part of that
layer's functionality.

In languages which have better inherent build
technology, Java, say, this kind of nonsense isn't
needed, but C is 32 years old by now, and its warts
are a lot more obvious than they were "back in the
day" when C was the best thing since sliced bread
and gained popularity exactly _because_ its warty
preprocessor technology let code be ported among
the much weirder variety of computer hardware word
sizes and OS library functionalities then prevalent.

Now that 32 bit and 64 bit technologies have won
out over the "divisible by three" word sizes, RAM
is abundant enough that code images don't need
trimming to the very smallest possible footprint via
conditional compilation, and Posix-compliant
libraries and interfaces thereto are a reasonable
expectation in _any_ OS, most of the need for the C
preprocessor capabilities is gone, but the habit of
depending on it for creating "pick from a menu"
software, as NetHack does with #ifdef wrapper
coding, that has nothing to do with code
portability, is too deeply entrenched in the C
programming community that C will ever be rid of the
mess it causes.

Probably your idle comment wasn't meant to elicit a
tutorial, but I'm a bit obsessive this way.

